WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:08.260
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.230
of "VM End-to-End," a show
where we have a VM skeptic

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.910
and a VM enthusiast
come together

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.970
to hash out all things
Google Cloud VM-related.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.642
Brian, thanks again for coming.

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:18.850
I'm excited for this episode.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.770
BRIAN DORSEY: Money
matters to everybody.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.863
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, exactly.

00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:24.280
Last episode, we
said, we're going

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.390
to have to talk about cost.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.015
What this is going
to do to my pockets?

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:30.320
And that's what I want to know,
because before this episode,

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.470
I actually went and
filled out a spreadsheet.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.595
I listed all of my resources--
all of my machines and CPUs.

00:00:36.595 --> 00:00:39.220
And then I went and saw how much
it would cost to just put that

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.450
over in the cloud.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:41.230
And--

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.130
BRIAN DORSEY: I know
where this is going.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:42.172
CARTER MORGAN: --I
don't know if you're

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:43.422
going to be surprised by this.

00:00:43.422 --> 00:00:46.757
It was way more expensive
than just running it on-prem.

00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:47.590
What's up with that?

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:48.965
BRIAN DORSEY: So
that does happen

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:51.520
when you do a straight
one-to-one mapping sometimes.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.090
And I think the key thing
is where Cloud usually

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.910
saves the most money
is through having

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.120
high utilization of the
machines you're actually

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.220
running at the time and changing
what you're running over time.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.510
So my question back
to you is, how did you

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.420
decide on those machine sizes
to put on the spreadsheet

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.847
and do the math out from?

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:13.180
CARTER MORGAN: I will be honest.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.990
I sized it based on if I was
purchasing a machine today,

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:18.830
I would need it to last
three to five years.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.010
So how much RAM
do I need to have

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.470
for it to be still relevant
three to five years from now?

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:24.860
How much space?

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.700
And then also, I
need these machines

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.150
to be able to handle peak
workloads that I might not

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.710
always be operating at.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.530
And so I had to be able to
have CPU and RAM that could

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.997
handle the worst-case scenario.

00:01:36.997 --> 00:01:38.830
BRIAN DORSEY: So most
days, they're probably

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.450
way overspecified.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:47.200
And then occasionally,
they're hitting their limits.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:47.770
And that's--

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:48.562
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:51.845
But they were cheaper
than the Cloud--

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:52.720
you know what I mean?

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.310
They were cheaper than the
Cloud VMs I was talking about.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.920
BRIAN DORSEY: That's where the
bulk of the savings-- and I

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.430
think there's some other
stuff I want to hit.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.460
But the bulk of the
savings come from this pay

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.410
for what you use concept.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.910
CARTER MORGAN: Pay
for what you use.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:05.950
I hear this.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:11.073
I hear this all the time and
I don't quite understand it.

00:02:11.073 --> 00:02:13.240
I just interrupted and you
were going to explain it.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.980
But I just hear
this phrase so much,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.210
and so I really want to know.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:16.450
I really didn't mean it.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.700
BRIAN DORSEY: So the simplest
is turn the machines off

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.825
when you're not using them.

00:02:19.825 --> 00:02:21.820
But I think it also is
a little bit confusing,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.770
because usage is a broad term.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.370
And we use it for actually
two different concepts

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.150
in Google Cloud.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.880
There's the pay for
specific work done.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.190
So if you're using a
cloud storage bucket,

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.370
actually saving an item in
the bucket or retrieving it.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.650
And then there's this
sense of reservations.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.640
So the way most of the
billing for the VM works is it

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.325
starts when you make the
API call to turn it on.

00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:47.950
And when we call to turn it off,
that's when the billing stops.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.683
So it's this reservation.

00:02:49.683 --> 00:02:51.850
CARTER MORGAN: So when they
talk down to the second,

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.020
pay for what you
use, that's what

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.010
they're saying in that case.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:54.100
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.642
And we actually do
bill to the second

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.460
with a 10-minute minimum.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.650
So when you do the math out for
a way overspecified machine,

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.760
it's going to sit at 5% usage
or something all the time.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.920
And all that extra is wasted.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.980
And that's what we want to
avoid to save the most money.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.510
The other thing, though--

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:12.670
CARTER MORGAN: That does
make a lot of sense.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.962
BRIAN DORSEY: --that I'm
going to jump on a little bit.

00:03:14.962 --> 00:03:16.840
So when you just do
the spreadsheet out,

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.930
you're only focusing
on the computers.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:22.705
And there's a lot more to it.

00:03:22.705 --> 00:03:26.192
So you have people's
time to set those up,

00:03:26.192 --> 00:03:28.150
to troubleshoot them when
something goes wrong,

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.910
to respond to failures
that might happen.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.980
You maybe overbuy
for redundancy.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.660
You've got to hook
them up to a network.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.480
You probably have some security
boundaries you want to enforce.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:38.860
Maybe you buy a load balancer.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.870
All this extra stuff
that is actually

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.210
baked into the
cost at the hourly

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.707
or the per-second
rate of a cloud VM.

00:03:45.707 --> 00:03:46.540
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.200
That really does
add up over time.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.280
And that's true.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.750
If I had to factor
in people-hours

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.780
and all the rest of this,
those costs in my spreadsheet

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.160
would go way up.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.410
Something I am
concerned with, though,

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.113
because it seems like a cloud VM
is a different mental paradigm.

00:04:04.113 --> 00:04:06.030
It's a shift in how we
have to think about it.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.300
So what are the hidden
costs or gotchas

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.933
that come up when
people are switching

00:04:09.933 --> 00:04:10.850
from on-prem to cloud?

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.000
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.320
And a lot of moving
parts, and we're

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.070
encouraging you to
turn things on and off,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.089
and maybe run test environments,
and this other stuff.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.370
You hear stories about I've
got this charge running

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.630
on some other service.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.850
I don't know where
it comes from.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.830
That I think we've got
a pretty good solution

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:25.663
for in Google Cloud.

00:04:25.663 --> 00:04:28.010
So we have a concept
of a project.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.660
And all of the resources
live in this project.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.020
And so if you
delete the project,

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.020
all the resources will get
turned off and not charged

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.055
for in the future.

00:04:38.055 --> 00:04:40.430
CARTER MORGAN: I want something
like that for all of my--

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:40.670
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh my gosh.

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:40.790
Me too.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:42.464
CARTER MORGAN:
--subscriptions in life.

00:04:42.464 --> 00:04:44.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.710
BRIAN DORSEY: That
would be amazing.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:50.365
There are some kind
of not obvious things

00:04:50.365 --> 00:04:51.740
that I think are
worth paying for

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:57.050
or that you might not think
to include in your estimates.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:59.360
So the worth it side,
I think it's worth

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.460
looking into the
support options,

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:05.433
because there's some really
highly trained, really awesome

00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:07.850
folks on our support team who
can help you out when things

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.855
get into troubleshooting mode.

00:05:09.855 --> 00:05:11.480
So I think that's
worth thinking about.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.370
The other one that
I think really

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:16.190
can be a surprise
is network traffic.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.520
So inbound networking is all
free and part of the deal.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.560
But you do pay for going out of
the cloud, what we call egress,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.280
of bandwidth.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.445
And it's different
depending on where it goes.

00:05:27.445 --> 00:05:28.820
CARTER MORGAN: So
different zones

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.208
might cost more, or from
one zone to another zone

00:05:31.208 --> 00:05:32.000
in another region--

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.430
BRIAN DORSEY: So
crossing regions

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.440
costs a little bit of
money, not as much.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.750
And then getting out
of cloud entirely--

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.097
so if you've got traffic
going to the United States,

00:05:41.097 --> 00:05:43.430
or to Asia, or Europe, there's
a slightly different rate

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:44.390
for each.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:45.140
CARTER MORGAN: It
seems like there's

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:46.990
a lot of different
moving pieces and parts.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:48.530
You touched on that earlier.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:51.440
How do I actually
go about estimating

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.920
what it's going to cost for me,
or how much of machine type 1

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.620
I'm going to need and machine
type 2, different tiers

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:58.190
of service?

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.767
How do you go about
estimating that?

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:01.850
BRIAN DORSEY: I have to,
unfortunately, admit this

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:05.750
is actually hard to do, partly
because there are so many

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:06.470
moving pieces.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.960
And a straight-up
multiply it out

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.490
in a spreadsheet is
a way overestimate.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.725
So how do we get more accurate?

00:06:13.725 --> 00:06:15.350
One of the things we
can do-- if you've

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.450
got a really good
understanding of a workload,

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.580
we have an online
calculator that

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.610
includes all of our discounts
and all the different services.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.010
And you can fill it
all in, and you'll

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:28.610
get a solid estimate of
what that would cost.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.250
Unfortunately, it's
difficult to get

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.200
to that level of
knowledge of a system

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.210
without running the system.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.710
And so what tends to
be the most accurate

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.840
is to run a mini
version of the system,

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.462
and log and monitor
that, and of do a spike,

00:06:46.462 --> 00:06:48.920
for either a shorter amount of
time or with fewer machines,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:52.120
just to see what is actually
all of the moving pieces.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.620
And then you can use that as a
base to multiply and estimate

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:55.490
from.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:58.790
CARTER MORGAN: That actually
makes a lot of sense.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:00.470
I guess one thing
I'm curious about,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.220
though-- because it makes sense
that if I'm only spinning up

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.560
machines that I'm
mostly utilizing,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.400
I'm going to save
money in that way.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.170
What about if I
have machines that

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.060
are running for a
long time, though,

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.160
or if I'm close to utilization?

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.960
Because it seems like
this is a rental type

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.880
model, where I'm renting,
renting, renting,

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.170
and never really owning.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:22.445
Are there any savings
related to that.

00:07:22.445 --> 00:07:23.487
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:07:23.487 --> 00:07:24.650
There's a whole bunch.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:28.400
And I think the main thing is--

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.070
and you're a Kubernetes guy.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.080
Why do containers exist at all?

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.430
I think it's to try to get high
utilization on the host machine

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.870
so that you get the most work.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.000
And the container keeps
each app in its own space

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.667
so you can put a lot
of different things

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:45.920
on the same box, and run the
box at 80% instead of 5%,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:46.710
things like that.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.950
So we have an extra
tool in Cloud VMs where,

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.280
instead of trying to fit a
lot of pieces in one box,

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.900
you can change the
shape of the box.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.420
So if you have a workload that
is very RAM-intensive or very

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.360
CPU-intensive, you can change
the shape of the machine

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.820
to fit your actual process.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.170
CARTER MORGAN: I
actually like that a lot.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.640
I'll throw in a random example.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.190
But if I was packing
a backpack, it's

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:15.190
like, if I can change the
shape of the dividers,

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:16.760
I can fit more in
instead of trying

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.430
to find odd-shaped items
to fit into the backpack.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.220
So that's one of the benefits
you get in the Cloud.

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:25.002
And that still lets you pay
less, because again, you're

00:08:25.002 --> 00:08:26.210
utilizing less space overall.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:26.870
BRIAN DORSEY: And those shapes--

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:27.390
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
that makes sense.

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:28.182
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:08:28.182 --> 00:08:29.902
CARTER MORGAN: That
doesn't really--

00:08:29.902 --> 00:08:31.610
well, I was going say
that doesn't really

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.900
touch on the rental model,
like this idea that we're

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.600
paying a lot, but we're never
really owning the machine.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.848
BRIAN DORSEY: To address
that more directly, it

00:08:40.848 --> 00:08:42.140
is always going to be a rental.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:45.780
But when you're using it for a
long time, it's more efficient.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.470
And so we automatically offer
what we call a sustained use

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:51.180
discount when that is the case.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.060
So if you're running
the same kinds of CPUs--

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.970
doesn't even need to be the same
machine, the same kinds of CPUs

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.800
over time, we'll
automatically give you

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.410
a discount of up to around 30%.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.300
And then if you know--

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.570
so there's a big deal there.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.720
And then if you know you've
got that base workload

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.350
over a long period of time, you
can essentially kind of reserve

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.260
that, a committed use discount
to pay ahead, and get an even

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.270
bigger discount on that.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.550
CARTER MORGAN: OK, so there's
a lot of savings there.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:17.600
There was another one--

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:18.410
I did a quick scan.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:21.000
I did a quick look, and
this one blew my mind.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.950
I do not understand it at all.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.780
They were called
preemptible VMs.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:25.780
Can tell me about those?

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:26.140
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:27.655
So if you imagine--
we were talking

00:09:27.655 --> 00:09:29.780
about this Tetris problem,
trying to fit things in.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:34.290
You've got a data
center-scale version of that.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:39.900
And so if we can run machines
in the corners, it's useful.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.140
So but we need to stay flexible.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.620
So we offer a machine,
preemptible type

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:49.020
or a preemptible mode,
basically, that fits in there.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.460
So if you know that you have
a workload that is either

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.710
relatively quick or
that can be restarted,

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.272
you can run it there and run
it at like a fifth of the cost.

00:09:57.272 --> 00:09:58.730
CARTER MORGAN: A
fifth of the cost?

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.300
BRIAN DORSEY: Like
20% of the cost.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.425
So the only difference
between a preemptible

00:10:02.425 --> 00:10:04.550
and a regular machine is
that it's guaranteed never

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:05.870
to last more than 24 hours.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:06.890
It will stop.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.090
And it might turn
off at any moment.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.420
So you have to be able
to restart your work.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:12.170
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.510
But for the types of
workloads that would fit for,

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:16.070
that's a huge savings.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:19.070
I guess another thing
I'm really interested in

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:20.460
is on that model--

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.230
again, I'm used
to maybe using 5%.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:28.190
And now we're moving
towards using 80%.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.230
What happens if I'm
not using anything

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.540
at all for a little
while, or I don't actually

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:33.770
have any activity going on?

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.145
BRIAN DORSEY:
That's even better.

00:10:35.145 --> 00:10:38.180
So if you stop a
VM, you only pay

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.790
for the disk usage and
nothing for the actual CPU,

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.540
memory part of it.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:46.135
So you can stop it and then turn
it back on whenever you want.

00:10:46.135 --> 00:10:47.760
And you can even do
that on a schedule.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.010
So if you know
three days a month,

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:50.450
this machine needs
to be working,

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:51.980
you can schedule
that ahead of time.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:52.897
CARTER MORGAN: Oh wow.

00:10:52.897 --> 00:10:55.530
OK, then what about for
things that I don't--

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:59.163
I can't predict, per
se, a spike in traffic

00:10:59.163 --> 00:11:00.830
for some sale or
something that went on,

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:02.617
some celebrity
mentioned my product.

00:11:02.617 --> 00:11:03.950
Is there any way to handle that?

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.617
BRIAN DORSEY: So the
most common pattern

00:11:05.617 --> 00:11:08.060
there I think is groups of
machines that are running

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:09.890
the same image
that work together

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:11.390
and all live behind
a load balancer.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:13.223
So you've got traffic
coming into one place,

00:11:13.223 --> 00:11:15.500
and it gets spread across
a couple of computers.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.150
And then you get more traffic,
and you add a few more

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:17.780
computers.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:20.870
Less traffic, you turn them off.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.810
So the managed instance
group can actually

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:27.230
auto-scale the size of the
group based on load or metrics

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:27.920
that you define.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.462
CARTER MORGAN: And
again, you're only

00:11:29.462 --> 00:11:31.100
paying once those VMs startup?

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.767
BRIAN DORSEY: They're
on and they exist.

00:11:35.038 --> 00:11:35.830
CARTER MORGAN: Wow.

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:38.140
So to recap, because this
actually seems really cool,

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:41.950
and it's changed the way I
think about pricing and cost,

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.922
because before, I was buying
for the next five years or so,

00:11:44.922 --> 00:11:46.630
plus playing for people
to manage things,

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.755
was paying for networking
and all the rest of that.

00:11:48.755 --> 00:11:51.280
And now with the cloud, it
seems like you're really

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:54.190
renting or buying for the
next five seconds or so.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:55.690
What am I using right now?

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.370
OK, let me try to
only pay for that.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:58.495
BRIAN DORSEY: I agree 100%.

00:11:58.495 --> 00:12:00.537
CARTER MORGAN: Does that
sound like a good recap?

00:12:00.537 --> 00:12:01.120
OK.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:03.022
So then, you know what?

00:12:03.022 --> 00:12:04.980
I kind of want to go and
actually try this out.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.938
I want to take my
